{"id":"h136","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Nitroglycerin (C_3 H_5 ( NO_3) _3) explodes according to the follow-ing reaction: 4C_3 H_5 (NO_3) _3 (l) \\rightarrow 12CO_2 (g) + 6N_2 (g) + O_2 (g) + 10H_2 O (g), producing only gaseous products. What is the total volume of gaseous products produced at standard temperature and pressure (STP) when 454 g of nitroglycerin explodes? The molecular weight of nitroglycerin is 227 g/mole.\nIs the correct answer \"325 liters\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"454 g of nitroglycerin at 227 g/mol equals 2.00 mol.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"4 mol of nitroglycerin produce 29 mol of gas (12+6+1+10).","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"2 mol of nitroglycerin therefore produce 14.5 mol of gas.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Because all products are stated to be gaseous, no water condensation correction is needed.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"At conventional STP (0 °C, 1 atm) one mole of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"14.5 mol × 22.4 L/mol is about 324.8 L, which rounds to 325 L.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Under IUPAC's 1982 STP definition (1 bar), molar volume is about 22.7 L, giving roughly 329 L instead.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Real-gas deviations are negligible, so ideal-gas treatment is fine.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The stoichiometry is unambiguous; the only real question is which molar volume convention applies.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The problem intends the classic textbook convention of 22.4 L/mol, so the answer is 325 L.","confidence":0.88,"lean":1,"theta":1.3823,"phi":